{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/lwritgvq","slideIndex":6,"position":{"left":1173.52,"top":23.04,"width":58.48,"height":58.48},"name":"Slide Number","placeholder":"slideNumber","text":"7","geometry":"ellipse","fillColor":"#FFFFFF","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Roboto","color":"#0B2545","bold":true,"alignment":"center"}}
{"kind":"table","id":"tb/bm98jid4","slideIndex":6,"position":{"left":57.6,"top":150,"width":1164.48,"height":454},"name":"Table 0","rows":6,"cols":2,"preview":"Item | Details"}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/gnmp4jqx","slideIndex":6,"text":"CNN-PT: supervised ImageNet ResNet-50 without adaptation. CNN-Common: three-class normal/pneumonia/COVID-19 classification. CNN-NIH: NIH multi-label classification. SwAV-PT: self-supervised ImageNet ResNet-50 without adaptation. SwAV-CLS: NIH multi-label classification from SwAV. SwAV-SupCon: weighted multi-label supervised contrastive learning from SwAV. The projection head is discarded for retrieval. Common-class adaptation used 4,545 balanced images and an 80:20 source-class-stratified split. NIH adaptation used 48,280 training and 12,266 validation images after target-patient exclusions, with 10,620 background images. Training used PyTorch 2.10.0+cu128 and two Tesla T4 GPUs. The five gallery seeds are not independent training runs. Paired query tests use Holm correction, with 72 planned model comparisons.\n\nSource: Chest_XRay_Retrieval_IEEE_Format.docx, supplied manuscript. Reference numbers follow the manuscript."}
{"kind":"slide","id":"sl/fu1gfa1s","slideIndex":7,"layout":{"id":"ly/14ra9krq","name":"ICAST Title Only"},"title":"Precision Falls as Cases Become Rarer","textShapes":3,"backgroundColor":"rgba(250,250,250,1)"}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/18byd4zy","slideIndex":7,"position":{"left":120,"top":0,"width":1040,"height":104.57},"name":"Text 1","placeholder":"title","text":"Precision Falls as Cases Become Rarer","geometry":"rect","style":{"fontSize":48,"typeface":"Roboto","color":"#FFFFFF","alignment":"center"}}
{"kind":"shape","id":"sh/ri9g7uhw","slideIndex":7,"position":{"left":825.6,"top":153.6,"width":396.48,"height":475.2},"name":"Takeaway card","geometry":"roundRect","fillColor":"#E8EEF6","style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Roboto","color":"#333333","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/6h0fypgb","slideIndex":7,"position":{"left":854.4,"top":182.4,"width":338.88,"height":422.4},"name":"Takeaway text","text":"All six models declined\n\nP@10 fell by 83.4%–91.9% from 5% to 0.5% prevalence across the 18 model–pathology pairs.\n\nEvery paired decline remained significant after Holm correction.\n\nAt 0.5%, random P@10 = 0.005.","geometry":"rect","style":{"fontSize":22,"typeface":"Roboto","color":"#0B2545","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/vq54b6lo","slideIndex":7,"position":{"left":1173.52,"top":23.04,"width":58.48,"height":58.48},"name":"Slide Number","placeholder":"slideNumber","text":"8","geometry":"ellipse","fillColor":"#FFFFFF","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":24,"typeface":"Roboto","color":"#0B2545","bold":true,"alignment":"center"}}
{"kind":"chart","id":"ch/exwn29or","slideIndex":7,"position":{"left":57.6,"top":151,"width":730,"height":465},"name":"","chartType":"bar","title":"SwAV-SupCon Precision@10"}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/fu1gfa1s","slideIndex":7,"text":"Endpoint values come from manuscript Table 2. The chart shows SwAV-SupCon, the highest mean model at every pathology-prevalence condition. All 18 included model-pathology pairs declined significantly for both metrics, Holm-adjusted p ≤ 2.30 × 10^-26. Across all models, absolute P@10 loss ranged from 0.0959 to 0.3480. The larger absolute loss for a stronger model does not establish poorer rare-case retrieval: SwAV-SupCon remained highest at both endpoints. The chart presents means across five gallery samples without uncertainty bars. Seed variation measures gallery sampling, not retraining. Pneumothorax uses SIIM queries and an NIH relevant gallery and is a cross-source condition.\n\nSource: Chest_XRay_Retrieval_IEEE_Format.docx, supplied manuscript. Reference numbers follow the manuscript."}
